Eladar wrote:
> I wouldn't say it's a "wonder"
> 
> When I asked just what nethack was coded in, i got the reply "archaic
> and badly written C".
> 
> Still, i think we can forgive it not being perfect due to the sheer
> volume of it!

Archaic, yes.  It uses pre-ansi prototypes, since it was written
pre-ansi.  Somewhat obscure, too; there are a lot of functions and
global structures that you need to know about before you'll be able
to understand what a function does.  I doubt there's a best practices
list out there that the Nethack source doesn't violate, and in
particular a lot of the modern beliefs about object orientation and
such being necessary for good good are ignored entirely.  And yet,
there are 156,000 lines of code and no real show-stopping bugs.
While the Nethack coding style is unusual, it apparently works.

And when you've read enough of it, and especially when you start
making changes yourself, things start to make more sense.  The names
are obscure, but consistent.  The globals (in particular struct you u)
are giant and convoluted, but everything you need to know about them
can usually be found in one header.  Some of those weird wrapper
functions, like mon_nam, a_monnam, Amonnam, An, makeplural, and pline
format information grammatically and handle all the memory use when
printing information to the screen, eliminating what would otherwise
be a huge likelihood of inconsistent formatting, buffer overruns, and
memory leaks.  A number of the headers are automatically generated
from information that's represented in a single place, again reducing
the chance of code going out-of-sync.  The source files themselves are
well thought out and modular; usually a single change won't need to
modify more than one or two files.

I agree that it looks hideous at first glance, but deeper down, it's
actually somewhat elegant.  I certainly prefer reading the Nethack
source over some of the more over-engineered projects that look fair
and feel foul.


Incidentally, the OP might find this useful: German Martin's
Beginner's Guide to Nethack Sources.
